                             Architecture\n     2\t\n     3\tThe schematic represents a high-cell-count Battery Management System (BMS) based on the **Texas Instruments                    
                             BQ79616PAPQ1** Automotive Battery Monitor and Balancer. This IC is capable of monitoring up to 16 series cells.\n     4\t\n     5\tThe design                  
                             includes:\n     6\t1.  **Main BMS IC (U1):** The BQ79616 handles cell voltage monitoring, internal cell balancing, and GPIO-based sensing.\n                   
                             7\t2.  **Cell Count Configuration:** A set of jumper resistors (currently DNP) allows the system to be configured for fewer than 16 cells by                   
                             shorting unused upper channels (`CBxx`/`VCxx`) to the highest active potential.\n     8\t3.  **Temperature Sensing Interface:** A bank of 8                    
                             thermistor dividers powered by the IC's `TSREF` output. These connect to the IC's GPIO pins via a configuration header (J4).\n     9\t4.  **Power              
                             & Communication:** The IC is powered from the battery stack (`PWR` net) and generates its own internal rails (`AVDD`, `CVDD`, `DVDD`). It features             
                             a daisy-chainable communication interface (`COMH`/`COML`) and a local UART/SPI interface (`RX`/`TX`).\n    10\t5.  **Auxiliary Measurement                     
                             Inputs:** The system includes two configurable paths for driving the `BBP` and `BBN` pins (Pins 64/63) of the BMS IC. These pins are typically                 
                             used for auxiliary voltage measurements. The two options are:\n    11\t    *   **Bus Bar Measurement (DNP):** Measures the voltage difference                  
                             between `BBP_CELL` and `BBN_CELL` via a differential RC filter.\n    12\t    *   **Current Sense Measurement (Active):** Measures the voltage from             
                             `SRP_S` and `SRN_S` (likely current sense shunt taps). This path is currently populated (direct connection).\n    13\t6.  **Communication                      
                             Interface:**\n    14\t    *   **Isolated Interface:** A digital isolator (U2) provides a galvanically isolated UART and Fault interface between                
                             the BMS IC (High Voltage) and an external host/microcontroller (Low Voltage).\n    15\t    *   **Direct Interface:** A header is provided for                  
                             direct, non-isolated connection to the BMS UART pins.\n    16\t\n    17\t7.  **NPN Power Supply:** An external NPN transistor circuit is provided              
                             to regulate the `LDOIN` supply for the BMS IC, reducing power dissipation within the IC. This circuit is powered from the main `PWR` rail.\n                   
                             18\t\n    19\t# Components Inventory\n    20\t\n    21\t**Integrated Circuits**\n    22\t*   **U1:** TI BQ79616PAPQ1 (16-Channel Battery                       
