'}}}]}, {'role': 'model', 'parts': [{'function_call': {'name': 'file_editor', 'args': {'command': 'view', 'path': '/home/pst/Documents/VHL_ANA/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana/resources/tsckt_operation_manual.md', 'security_risk': 'LOW'}}, 'thoughtSignature': 'c2tpcF90aG91Z2h0X3NpZ25hdHVyZV92YWxpZGF0b3I='}]}, {'parts': [{'function_response': {'name': 'file_editor', 'response': {'content': 'Here\'s the result of running `cat -n` on /home/pst/Documents/VHL_ANA/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana/resources/tsckt_operation_manual.md:\n     1\t# `tscircuit` Operational Guide: Component Creation, Modules & Assembly\n     2\t\n     3\t## Overview\n     4\t\n     5\tThis guide provides the necessary instructions to create custom components from datasheets, organize them into modular groups, and assemble them into a functional circuit. It relies on explicit pin mapping, modular grouping with auto-layout, and net-based connectivity.\n     6\t\n     7\t## 1. Creating Custom Components\n     8\t\n     9\tTo create a new component (e.g., an IC), you must wrap the component definition in a `.tsx` file.\n    10\t\n    11\t### A. Importing Footprints\n    12\t\n    13\t`tscircuit` supports direct import of KiCAD (`.kicad_mod`) footprints.\n    14\t\n    15\t  * **Source:** Obtain the correct `.kicad_mod` file.\n    16\t  * **Syntax:** `import customFootprint from "./path/to/footprint.kicad_mod"`\n    17\t\n    18\t### B. Defining the Component Symbol (MANDATORY PROPS)\n    19\t\n    20\tUse the `<chip />` primitive.\n    21\t\n    22\t  * **Footprint:** Pass the imported variable to the `footprint` prop.\n    23\t  * **Pin Mapping:** Use `pinLabels` to map physical pin numbers to datasheet names.\n    24\t  * **Mandatory Prop Handling:**\n    25\t      * **Rule:** Your component **MUST** extend `CommonLayoutProps` and spread `{...props}` onto the underlying `<chip />`.\n    26\t      * **Reason:** This allows the parent circuit to control positioning (`schX`, `pcbX`) and rotation.\n    27\t\n    28\t### C. Standard Component Template\n    29\t\n    30\t**File:** `lib/Components/GenericControllerIC.tsx`\n    31\t\n    32\t```typescript\n    33\timport type { CommonLayoutProps } from "tscircuit"\n    34\timport QFP_Footprint from "../footprints/QFP32_Generic.kicad_mod"\n    35\t\n    36\t// 1. MANDATORY: Interface must extend CommonLayoutProps\n    37\tinterface Props extends CommonLayoutProps {\n    38\t  name: string\n    39\t}\n    40\t\n    41\texport const GenericControllerIC = (props: Props) => {\n    42\t  return (\n    43\t    <chip\n    44\t      footprint={QFP_Footprint}\n    45\t      pinLabels={{\n    46\t        pin1: "VDD",\n    47\t        pin2: "GND",\n    48\t        // ... mappings\n    49\t        pin32: "VSS"\n    50\t      }}\n    51\t      // 2. CRITICAL: Spread props to enable positioning\n    52\t      {...props}\n    53\t    />\n    54\t   )\n    55\t}\n    56\t```\n    57\t\n    58\t-----\n    59\t\n    60\t## 2. Modular Circuit Design with `<group />`\n    61\t\n    62\tThe `<group />` element is a container used to organize multiple components into a single block. It is highly recommended to use `<group>` with **Flex** or **Grid** props to handle layout automatically, rather than calculating manual coordinates for every sub-component.\n    63\t\n    64\t### A. Creating a Reusable Module\n    65\t\n    66\tWrap related components and traces inside a `<group />`.\n    67\t\n    68\t  * **Mandatory Prop Handling:** Your Module **MUST** accept `CommonLayoutProps` and spread them onto the root `<group>` tag.\n    69\t\n    70\t**File:** `lib/Modules/PowerInput.tsx`\n    71\t\n    72\t```typescript\n    73\timport type { CommonLayoutProps } from "tscircuit"\n    74\t\n    75\tinterface Props extends CommonLayoutProps {\n    76\t  namePrefix?: string \n    77\t}\n    78\t\n    79\texport const PowerInputModule = (props: Props) => {\n    80\t  return (\n    81\t    // CRITICAL: Spread props here to allow moving the whole group\n    82\t    <group {...props}>\n    83\t      {/* Passives must have a footprint and name */}\n    84\t      <resistor name="R_Fuse" resistance="10" footprint="0603" schX={0} schY={0} />\n    85\t      <capacitor name="C_Bulk" capacitance="100uF" footprint="1206" schX={10} schY={0} />\n    86\t      \n    87\t      {/* Internal connections */}\n    88\t      <trace from=".R_Fuse > .pin2" to=".C_Bulk > .pos" />\n    89\t    </group>\n    90\t  )\n    91\t}\n    92\t```\n    93\t\n    94\t### B. Auto-Layout (Grid & Flex)\n    95\t\n    96\tUse these props on the `<group>` tag to arrange children automatically without manual X/Y math.\n    97\t\n    98\t  * **Schematic Layout:** `schFlex`, `schFlexDirection` (row/column), `schGap`.\n    99\t  * **PCB Layout:** `pcbGrid`, `pcbGridCols`, `pcbGridGap`.\n   100\t\n   101\t**Example (Auto-arranged LEDs):**\n   102\t\n   103\t```typescript\n   104\t<group \n   105\t  schFlex schFlexDirection="column" schGap="2mm" \n   106\t  pcbGrid pcbGridCols={2} pcbGridGap="1mm"\n   107\t  {...props}\n   108\t>\n   109\t    <led name="LED1" color="red" footprint="0603" />\n   110\t    <led name="LED2" color="green" footprint="0603" />\n   111\t    <led name="LED3" color="blue" footprint="0603" />\n   112\t    <led name="LED4" color="yellow" footprint="0603" />\n   113\t</group>\n   114\t```\n   115\t\n   116\t-----\n   117\t\n   118\t## 3. Circuit Assembly and Connectivity\n   119\t\n   120\t### A. Instantiation & Placement\n   121\t\n   122\t  * **Container:** `<board width="..." height="...">`.\n   123\t  * **Primitives:** Use built-in lowercase elements (e.g., `<resistor />`, `<capacitor />`) directly. **Do not import them.**\n   124\t  * **Positioning:** Use `schX`/`schY` (schematic) and `pcbX`/`pcbY` (PCB) on components or groups.\n   125\t\n   126\t### B. Connectivity\n   127\t\n   128\t  * **Direct Trace:** `<trace from=".U1 > .pin1" to=".R1 > .pin2" />`\n   129\t  * **Power Nets:** Use `<net name="VCC" />`. Connect pins to `net.VCC`.\n   130\t\n   131\t### C. Assembly Template\n   132\t\n   133\t**File:** `index.tsx`\n   134\t\n   135\t```typescript\n   136\timport { GenericControllerIC } from "./lib/Components/GenericControllerIC"\n   137\timport { PowerInputModule } from "./lib/Modules/PowerInput"\n   138\t\n   139\texport default () => {\n   140\t  return (\n   141\t    <board width="100mm" height="80mm">\n   142\t      <net name="VCC" />\n   143\t      <net name="GND" />\n   144\t\n   145\t      {/* 1. Place the Power Module */}\n   146\t      <PowerInputModule schX={-20} schY={0} pcbX={-20} pcbY={10} />\n   147\t\n   148\t      {/* 2. Place the Main IC */}\n   149\t      <GenericControllerIC name="U_Main" schX={20} schY={0} pcbX={20} pcbY={10} />\n   150\t\n   151\t      {/* 3. Place a decoupling capacitor (Standard Element) */}\n   152\t      <capacitor \n   153\t         name="C_Decouple" \n   154\t         capacitance="100nF" \n   155\t         footprint="0402" \n   156\t         schX={20} schY={10} \n   157\t      />\n   158\t\n   159\t      {/* 4. Global Connections */}\n   160\t      <trace from=".U_Main > .VDD" to="net.VCC" />\n   161\t      <trace from=".U_Main > .GND" to="net.GND" />\n   162\t      <trace from=".C_Decouple > .pin1" to="net.VCC" />\n   163\t      <trace from=".C_Decouple > .pin2" to="net.GND" />\n   164\t    </board>\n   165\t  )\n   166\t}\n   167\t```\n   168\t\n   169\t-----\n   170\t\n   171\t## 4. Built-in Elements in `tscircuit`\n   172\t\n   173\tThese elements are available globally. You do not need to import them.\n   174\t\n   175\t| Element | Description/Usage |\n   176\t| :--- | :--- |\n   177\t| `<board />` | Root container for all components and traces. |\n   178\t| `<chip />` | Generic component for ICs with custom footprints/pin mappings. |\n   179\t| `<resistor />` | Limits current. **Requires `footprint` (e.g., "0402"), `resistance`, `name`.** |\n   180\t| `<capacitor />` | Stores energy/filters. **Requires `footprint`, `capacitance`, `name`.** |\n   181\t| `<inductor />` | Stores energy in magnetic field. **Requires `footprint`, `inductance`, `name`.** |\n   182\t| `<diode />` | Rectification/protection. **Requires `footprint`, `name`.** |\n   183\t| `<led />` | Light emitting diode. **Requires `footprint`, `color`, `name`.** |\n   184\t| `<transistor />` | Generic three-terminal amplification/switching device. |\n   185\t| `<mosfet />` | Field-effect transistor (Switching). |\n   186\t| `<crystal />` | Clock signal source. |\n   187\t| `<switch />` / `<pushbutton />` | Mechanical connection controls. |\n   188\t| `<jumper />` / `<solderjumper />` | Configuration bridges. |\n   189\t| `<trace />` | Connects two points (`from`, `to`). |\n   190\t| `<net />` | Groups traces into a named node (e.g., "VCC", "GND"). |\n   191\t| `<via />` | Plated hole connecting PCB layers. |\n   192\t| `<footprint />` | Defines physical layout (used internally or for custom parts). |\n   193\t| `<pinheader />` | Connector for external interfaces. |\n   194\t| `<group />` | Container for organizing elements. Supports Flex/Grid layouts. |\n   195\t| `<subcircuit />` | Tightly coupled functional block with isolated nets/autorouting. |\n   196\t| `<testpoint />` | Layout feature for electrical testing. |\n   197\t| `<hole />` | Non-conductive mounting hole. |\n   198\t\n   199\t-----\n   200\t\n   201\t## 5. Agent Instructions for "Text-to-Circuit"\n   202\t\n   203\tWhen generating code:\n   204\t\n   205\t1.  **Define Nets:** If standard rails (VCC, GND) are needed, instantiate `<net />` elements first.\n   206\t2.  **Passives:** Use standard tags (e.g., `<resistor />`) but **ALWAYS** specify a `footprint` (e.g., "0402", "0603") and a `name`.\n   207\t3.  **Modules:** Use `<group>` with `schFlex`/`pcbGrid` props to arrange components automatically.\n   208\t4.  **Mandatory Props:** Ensure ALL custom components and groups accept `CommonLayoutProps` and spread `{...props}` to the root element.\n   209\t5.  **Connect:** Use point-to-point traces for signals and nets for power.\n'}}}]}, {'role': 'model', 'parts': [{'function_call': {'name': 'think', 'args': {'thought': "I will start by creating the `index.tsx` file in the `ana/output` directory. I'll follow the BFS-DC method, starting with the main IC (U1: BQ79616PAPR) and then expanding to its connected components.\n\nAnchor 1: U1 (BQ79616PAPR)\nAnchor 2: U2 (ISO7342FCQDWRQ1)\n\nI'll organize the circuit into groups for better structure:\n1. Main IC & Decoupling\n2. NPN Supply (Q1)\n3. Temperature Sensing (GPIOs)\n4. Communication (Isolated & Direct)\n5. Auxiliary Measurement (BBP/BBN)\n6. Cell Configuration (Jumpers)\n\nI'll use the local library components as identified in the SCUD.\n\nWait, I need to check the pinout of the components in the library to ensure correct connectivity. I'll view the library files."}}, 'thoughtSignature': 'c2tpcF90aG91Z2h0X3NpZ25hdHVyZV92YWxpZGF0b3I='}]}, {'parts': [{'function_response': {'name': 'think', 'response': {'content': 'Your thought has been logged.'}}}]}], 'system_instruction': {'parts': [{'text': '# **SYSTEM PROMPT — ANA (ANalog Circuit Design Agent)**\n\n---\n\n## 0. Identity & Scope\n\nYou are **ANA — the ANalog Circuit Design Agent**, operating inside the **Virtual Hardware Laboratory (VHL)**.\n\nYour role is **strictly bounded**:\n\n* You **construct** analog circuit definitions in **tscircuit (.tsx)** format\n* You **do not** interpret, fix, or reason about system behavior\n* You **propose artifacts**; **VHL decides acceptance or rejection**\n\nYou are **not** a general problem solver.\nYou are a **deterministic circuit constructor operating under hard constraints**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. System Context (Read Carefully)\n\nVHL is an **agentic embedded hardware development infrastructure**.\n\n### Pipeline Position\n\nYou operate **after**:\n\n1. **Archy** has generated a\n   **Shared Circuit Understanding Document (SCUD)**\n2. **Librarian** has ensured all required components are available in the\n   **VHL Component Library**\n\nYour task is to convert **SCUD → tscircuit (.tsx)**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Ground Truth & Information Hierarchy\n\nYou must follow this **strict precedence order**:\n\n1. **SCUD** — Primary, contractual source\n2. **Schematic Images** — Absolute ground truth (consult *only if unavoidable*)\n3. **tscircuit Operational Manual** — Usage reference\n4. **VHL Library Listings** — Availability confirmation\n\nRules:\n\n* **Never infer connectivity not present in SCUD**\n* **Never invent components**\n* **Never encode IC-internal behavior**\n* **Ambiguity must propagate as failure**, not imagination\n\nSCUD is a **contract**, not an explanation.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Library Interaction Rules\n\nYou may use **VHL Librarian tools** only to:\n\n* `list_local_components`\n* observe what is already available\n\n### Missing Component Policy\n\nIf a required component is missing:\n\n1. **Attempt replacement** ONLY if:\n\n   * Schematic symbol (pin mapping) matches\n   * Footprint matches\n   * Component name clearly implies the intended device\n2. Otherwise:\n\n   * **STOP**\n   * Clearly state the reason\n   * This is a **human intervention request**\n   * Do not continue\n\nYou must **never fabricate components**.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Circuit Construction Strategy (MANDATORY)\n\nYou must follow the **BFS-DC (Breadth-First Search with Degree Centrality)** method.\n\n### Step 1 — Identify Anchor Components\n\n* Primary ICs (MCUs, ASICs, PMICs, etc.)\n* Highest connectivity degree\n* These define the circuit’s constraint backbone\n\n### Step 2 — BFS Expansion\n\nFor each anchor:\n\n1. Model the anchor completely\n2. Layer-1: Components directly connected to the anchor\n3. Layer-2: Components connected to Layer-1\n4. Continue until the subgraph is complete\n5. Move to the next anchor\n\n### Step 3 — Isolated Components\n\nExplicitly model any remaining isolated components\n\nRules:\n\n* One local subgraph at a time\n* No global reasoning\n* Incremental construction only\n\n---\n\n## 5. Coding Rules (tscircuit)\n\n* Use **only the minimal, proven subset** of tscircuit primitives\n* Prefer robustness over elegance\n* Avoid experimental or undocumented features\n* Write **one monolithic .tsx file per SCUD**\n\nYou may construct and edit the file **locally** using a text editor.\n\nVHL is **not involved** during construction.\n\n---\n\n## 6. VHL ANA Process (VAP) — Evaluation Interface\n\nVAP is a **process**, not a function.\n\n### Available Actions\n\n1. **VAP_init**\n\n   * Inputs:\n\n     * circuit name\n     * full `.tsx` code (text)\n   * Effect:\n\n     * Starts evaluation\n     * Writes a *temporary* file\n\n2. **VAP_status**\n\n   * Returns:\n\n     * process state\n     * full logs (stdout/stderr)\n   * Uses **long-wait polling**\n\n### Critical Semantics\n\n* Evaluation time is **unbounded**\n* Timeout is **not a diagnosis**\n* Timeout is a **decision boundary**\n* VAP outcomes are **atomic**:\n\n  * ACCEPT\n  * REJECT\n\nYou must:\n\n* Poll until completion\n* Read logs\n* Decide your next action **outside** VHL\n\n---\n\n## 7. Failure Handling Discipline\n\nIf VAP **REJECTS**:\n\n* Inspect logs\n* Modify the `.tsx` file\n* Start a **new** VAP run\n\nRules:\n\n* No automatic retries\n* No interpretation by VHL\n* No partial persistence\n* Each attempt is isolated and disposable\n\nIf failure persists and ambiguity is structural:\n\n* **STOP**\n* Explicitly state uncertainty\n* Request human intervention\n\n---\n\n## 8. Success Semantics\n\nIf VAP **ACCEPTS**:\n\n* The `.tsx` file is moved to VHL’s circuit directory\n* This is the **only success signal**\n* No additional confirmation is required\n\nYou may then terminate successfully.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Architectural Invariants (You Must Obey These)\n\nYou must never violate:\n\n* **Agents propose; systems decide**\n* **No persistence without acceptance**\n* **State drives control; logs drive reasoning**\n* **Time is a system concern**\n* **All unpredictable operations are processes**\n* **VHL is a tool, not an intelligence**\n\nIf you cannot proceed without violating an invariant:\n\n* **STOP**\n\n---\n\n## 10. Explicitly Forbidden Behaviors\n\nYou must NOT:\n\n* Infer intent from failures\n* Diagnose timeouts\n* Auto-fix without evidence\n* Invent nets, pins, or components\n* Encode layout strategy\n* Speculate beyond SCUD\n* Bypass VAP\n* Treat warnings as acceptance\n* Treat logs as control signals\n\n---\n\n## 11. Your Termination Conditions\n\nYou must terminate when:\n\n1. VAP ACCEPTS → **Success**\n2. Required component missing → **Human intervention**\n3. SCUD ambiguity blocks construction → **Human intervention**\n4. Repeated deterministic failure reveals contract inconsistency → **Human intervention**\n\nStopping is **correct behavior**.\n\n---\n\n## Final Principle\n\n> **You are a constructor, not a judge.**\n> **VHL enforces reality.**\n> **You respect the boundary.**'}]}, 'tools': [{'function_declarations': [{'name': 'terminal', 'description': 'Execute a bash command in the terminal within a persistent shell session.\n\n\n### Command Execution\n* One command at a time: You can only execute one bash command at a time. If you need to run multiple commands sequentially, use `&&` or `;` to chain them together.\n* Persistent session: Commands execute in a persistent shell session where environment variables, virtual environments, and working directory persist between commands.\n* Soft timeout: Commands have a soft timeout of 10 seconds, once that\'s reached, you have the option to continue or interrupt the command (see section below for details)\n* Shell options: Do NOT use `set -e`, `set -eu`, or `set -euo pipefail` in shell scripts or commands in this environment. The runtime may not support them and can cause unusable shell sessions. If you want to run multi-line bash commands, write the commands to a file and then run it, instead.\n\n### Long-running Commands\n* For commands that may run indefinitely, run them in the background and redirect output to a file, e.g. `python3 app.py > server.log 2>&1 &`.\n* For commands that may run for a long time (e.g. installation or testing commands), or commands that run for a fixed amount of time (e.g. sleep), you should set the "timeout" parameter of your function call to an appropriate value.\n* If a bash command returns exit code `-1`, this means the process hit the soft timeout and is not yet finished. By setting `is_input` to `true`, you can:\n  - Send empty `command` to retrieve additional logs\n  - Send text (set `command` to the text) to STDIN of the running process\n  - Send control commands like `C-c` (Ctrl+C), `C-d` (Ctrl+D), or `C-z` (Ctrl+Z) to interrupt the process\n  - If you do C-c, you can re-start the process with a longer "timeout" parameter to let it run to completion\n\n### Best Practices\n* Directory verification: Before creating new directories or files, first verify the parent directory exists and is the correct location.\n* Directory management: Try to maintain working directory by using absolute paths and avoiding excessive use of `cd`.\n\n### Output Handling\n* Output truncation: If the output exceeds a maximum length, it will be truncated before being returned.\n\n### Terminal Reset\n* Terminal reset: If the terminal becomes unresponsive, you can set the "reset" parameter to `true` to create a new terminal session. This will terminate the current session and start fresh.\n* Warning: Resetting the terminal will lose all previously set environment variables, working directory changes, and any running processes. Use this only when the terminal stops responding to commands.\n', 'parameters': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'command': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The bash command to execute. Can be empty string to view additional logs when previous exit code is `-1`. Can be `C-c` (Ctrl+C) to interrupt the currently running process. Note: You can only execute one bash command at a time. If you need to run multiple commands sequentially, you can use `&&` or `;` to chain them together.'}, 'is_input': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'If True, the command is an input to the running process. If False, the command is a bash command to be executed in the terminal. Default is False.'}, 'timeout': {'type': 'number', 'description': 'Optional. Sets a maximum time limit (in seconds) for running the command. If the command takes longer than this limit, you’ll be asked whether to continue or stop it. If you don’t set a value, the command will instead pause and ask for confirmation when it produces no new output for 30 seconds. Use a higher value if the command is expected to take a long time (like installation or testing), or if it has a known fixed duration (like sleep).'}, 'reset': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'If True, reset the terminal by creating a new session. Use this only when the terminal becomes unresponsive. Note that all previously set environment variables and session state will be lost after reset. Cannot be used with is_input=True.'}, 'security_risk': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Security risk levels for actions.\n\nBased on OpenHands security risk levels but adapted for agent-sdk.\nInteger values allow for easy comparison and ordering.', 'enum': ['UNKNOWN', 'LOW', 'MEDIUM', 'HIGH']}}, 'required': ['command', 'security_risk']}}, {'name': 'file_editor', 'description': "Custom editing tool for viewing, creating and editing files in plain-text format\n* State is persistent across command calls and discussions with the user\n* If `path` is an image file (.png, .jpg, .jpeg, .gif, .webp, .bmp), `view` displays the image content\n* If `path` is a text file, `view` displays the result of applying `cat -n`. If `path` is a directory, `view` lists non-hidden files and directories up to 2 levels deep\n* The `create` command cannot be used if the specified `path` already exists as a file\n* If a `command` generates a long output, it will be truncated and marked with `<response clipped>`\n* The `undo_edit` command will revert the last edit made to the file at `path`\n* This tool can be used for creating and editing files in plain-text format.\n\n\nBefore using this tool:\n1. Use the view tool to understand the file's contents and context\n2. Verify the directory path is correct (only applicable when creating new files):\n   - Use the view tool to verify the parent directory exists and is the correct location\n\nWhen making edits:\n   - Ensure the edit results in idiomatic, correct code\n   - Do not leave the code in a broken state\n   - Always use absolute file paths (starting with /)\n\nCRITICAL REQUIREMENTS FOR USING THIS TOOL:\n\n1. EXACT MATCHING: The `old_str` parameter must match EXACTLY one or more consecutive lines from the file, including all whitespace and indentation. The tool will fail if `old_str` matches multiple locations or doesn't match exactly with the file content.\n\n2. UNIQUENESS: The `old_str` must uniquely identify a single instance in the file:\n   - Include sufficient context before and after the change point (3-5 lines recommended)\n   - If not unique, the replacement will not be performed\n\n3. REPLACEMENT: The `new_str` parameter should contain the edited lines that replace the `old_str`. Both strings must be different.\n\nRemember: when making multiple file edits in a row to the same file, you should prefer to send all edits in a single message with multiple calls to this tool, rather than multiple messages with a single call each.\n\n\nYour current working directory is: /home/pst/Documents/VHL_ANA/virtual_hardware_laboratary\nWhen exploring project structure, start with this directory instead of the root filesystem.", 'parameters': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'command': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The commands to run. Allowed options are: `view`, `create`, `str_replace`, `insert`, `undo_edit`.', 'enum': ['view', 'create', 'str_replace', 'insert', 'undo_edit']}, 'path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Absolute path to file or directory.'}, 'file_text': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Required parameter of `create` command, with the content of the file to be created.'}, 'old_str': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Required parameter of `str_replace` command containing the string in `path` to replace.'}, 'new_str': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Optional parameter of `str_replace` command containing the new string (if not given, no string will be added). Required parameter of `insert` command containing the string to insert.'}, 'insert_line': {'type': 'integer', 'description': 'Required parameter of `insert` command. The `new_str` will be inserted AFTER the line `insert_line` of `path`.'}, 'view_range': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'integer'}, 'description': 'Optional parameter of `view` command when `path` points to a file. If none is given, the full file is shown. If provided, the file will be shown in the indicated line number range, e.g. [11, 12] will show lines 11 and 12. Indexing at 1 to start. Setting `[start_line, -1]` shows all lines from `start_line` to the end of the file.'}, 'security_risk': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Security risk levels for actions.\n\nBased on OpenHands security risk levels but adapted for agent-sdk.\nInteger values allow for easy comparison and ordering.', 'enum': ['UNKNOWN', 'LOW', 'MEDIUM', 'HIGH']}}, 'required': ['command', 'path', 'security_risk']}}, {'name': 'VHL_Library_add_component', 'description': 'Add or update a TSX component or KiCAD footprint (.kicad_mod) in the local library. Validation is automatic.', 'parameters': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'component_name': {'type': 'string', 'description': "Logical name of the component (e.g. 'MyResistor') or filename with extension (e.g. 'footprint.kicad_mod')"}, 'file_content': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Complete file content (TSX or KiCAD footprint)'}, 'security_risk': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Security risk levels for actions.\n\nBased on OpenHands security risk levels but adapted for agent-sdk.\nInteger values allow for easy comparison and ordering.', 'enum': ['UNKNOWN', 'LOW', 'MEDIUM', 'HIGH']}}, 'required': ['component_name', 'file_content', 'security_risk']}}, {'name': 'VHL_Library_list_local_components', 'description': 'List components available in the local library.', 'parameters': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'security_risk': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Security risk levels for actions.\n\nBased on OpenHands security risk levels but adapted for agent-sdk.\nInteger values allow for easy comparison and ordering.', 'enum': ['UNKNOWN', 'LOW', 'MEDIUM', 'HIGH']}}, 'required': ['security_risk']}}, {'name': 'VHL_Library_resolve_component_start', 'description': "PROCESS STEP 1: Start a component resolution process. This is a NON-BLOCKING operation that initiates a background process to resolve a hardware component. The process first checks the local library, then attempts to import from the global registry if not found. Returns immediately with a task_id and initial state 'checking_local'. You MUST poll resolve_component_status to monitor progress. Only ONE resolution task can be active at a time - attempting to start a second task will fail. Use this when you need to resolve/import a component that may not exist locally.", 'parameters': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'component_name': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Name of the component to resolve.'}, 'security_risk': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Security risk levels for actions.\n\nBased on OpenHands security risk levels but adapted for agent-sdk.\nInteger values allow for easy comparison and ordering.', 'enum': ['UNKNOWN', 'LOW', 'MEDIUM', 'HIGH']}}, 'required': ['component_name', 'security_risk']}}, {'name': 'VHL_Library_resolve_component_status', 'description': "PROCESS STEP 2: Poll the current state of an active resolution task. Returns a status object with 'state' field that can be: 'checking_local' (